4. Controller-side CAPWAP debugging

On USADC5520WLC02:
config paging disable
config session timeout 0

Then target the AP:
debug mac addr xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
debug capwap events enable
debug capwap errors enable
debug dtls all enable


Cisco specifically recommends CAPWAP event/error debugging when an AP won’t join.

May also need:
debug capwap packet enable
debug pm pki enable

debug pm pki enable becomes especially useful if the AP reaches the controller but certificate validation is failing.

ALWAYS turn debugging off afterward

debug disable-all

Don’t leave those running.


5. Commands on the AP itself

Once you’re SSH’d into WAPGOZ92_24, start with these.

Which WLC am I actually joined to?

show capwap client config
This is probably the single most important AP-side command.

Look for controller/WLC information such as:
mwarName
mwarIPAddress
Cisco documents show capwap client config as displaying the AP’s CAPWAP configuration, including controller information. 

Depending on the AP software, also try:
show capwap client rcb
show capwap client ha
show capwap client timers
show capwap client traffic
Cisco’s newer AP command reference documents these CAPWAP client views for configuration, HA state, timers, traffic, etc.


AP network configuration
show ip interface brief
show interfaces
show ip route
You’re checking that the AP still has proper addressing and a route toward 172.28.182.214.

Also:
ping 172.28.182.214
This is huge.

If the AP itself cannot ping the restored primary WLC, don’t bother forcing failback yet—you have a reachability/routing problem.
If it can ping 172.28.182.214, you’ve eliminated a major possibility.


AP logging
Already ran these:
show logging
show tech
Also useful:
show clock

Timestamps matter when matching AP logs against WLC debugs.
Cisco recommends collecting show tech, show logging, CAPWAP state and related AP information before/after troubleshooting.


