7. How to test failback with ONE AP
Before touching hundreds of APs, use WAPGOZ92_24.
First verify on the secondary:
show ap config general WAPGOZ92_24

Make sure primary really says:
USADC5520WLC02
172.28.182.214

Then make sure the AP can reach ADC:
ping 172.28.182.214
Then start TAC’s debugs on ADC and the AP.

Finally, from the secondary WLC:
config ap reset WAPGOZ92_24
Cisco documents config ap reset <AP> as the AireOS command to reset an individual lightweight AP.

Then watch:
ADC:
grep include "WAPGOZ92_24" "show ap summary"

Secondary:
grep include "WAPGOZ92_24" "show ap summary"
If everything is correct, you’re looking to see it disappear from CNJ, discover ADC, and join ADC.


8. Can you reload ALL APs to force failback?

Technically, AireOS gives us mechanisms to reset APs, but I would not run a bulk reset during the daytime just to force failback.
Ron already told us the planned failback can be initiated after 7 PM EST. That’s the appropriate window because forcing hundreds of APs off CNJ will interrupt wireless service.

Cisco’s documented individual command is:
config ap reset <AP_NAME>

Before considering anything bulk, have TAC/Ron confirm the exact method they want. Don’t write a shell/expect loop to reset 300–600 APs from the controller. It gives you much less control over impact and recovery.

Today we only need to prove:
one ADC AP
    ↓
currently CNJ
    ↓
primary = ADC
    ↓
AP can reach ADC
    ↓
reset AP
    ↓
AP discovers ADC
    ↓
AP joins ADC

Once that’s successful, you know the failback mechanism works.


